    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d329">

                <pb facs="0303" n="291" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg291"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">891.00/1854: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Iran</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Dreyfus</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tehran</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-04-03T16:00:00+03:25">April 3, 1942—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 8:16 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>95. Prime Minister yesterday in conference with Ambassador Standley<anchor n="6"
                        xml:id="d329-f1"/> reiterated Iran’s desire to continue friendly relations
                    with United States and expressed hope that American help in form of advances and
                    goods would be forthcoming now and in period after war. An indication of Iran’s
                    friendship he said was to be found in decision to return schools<anchor n="7"
                        xml:id="d329-f2"/> and action now being taken to expel Japanese Legation
                    which Iranian Government had refused to do at British request several months
                    ago. He stated that he will instruct his Ambassador to Russia to cooperate
                    closely with Standley as he did with Steinhardt<anchor n="8" xml:id="d329-f3"/>
                    and that he himself will keep in close touch with me. He evinced little concern
                    over situation in southern Iran where police and Government are functioning but
                    believed situation in Russian occupied areas in north where police and other
                    authorities are not permitted to operate gives definite cause for alarm. He
                    expressed belief that tribes in north are loyal with exception of Kurds whose
                    disaffection and susceptibility to Soviet influence might offer an eventual
                    excuse for Soviets to take over region.<anchor n="9" xml:id="d329-f4"/> Iranian
                    Government he declared is concerned about status of northern Iran after war but
                    in this regard places great confidence in Atlantic Charter.<anchor n="10"
                        xml:id="d329-f5"/>
                </p>
                <p>He ended by asking Standley to bear above points in mind in his dealings with
                    Russia.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dreyfus</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
